and he has quite a fascinating story to tell you&nbsp;
about witnessing a political rally in October of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:18.660
1884. this was a few weeks before the election&nbsp;
that made Grover Cleveland the 22nd president of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:24.000
the United States now some of you may have heard&nbsp;
of Carl Sandburg before he was a well-known poet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.500
and biographer in the first half of the 20th&nbsp;
century and he lived in a small town called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.020
Galesburg Illinois which is where the political&nbsp;
rally that he speaks of took place at that time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:40.320
in the 1880s Illinois was predominantly Republican&nbsp;
and so you'll hear Mr Sandburg describe how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.720
crowd at the rally was mostly chanting for a man&nbsp;
named James G Blaine the Republican nominee for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.820
the presidency at the time the 1884 presidential&nbsp;
election was highly contentious and marked by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:56.280
strong hostility toward both candidates Cleveland&nbsp;
faced accusations of fathering an illegitimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.560
child while James G Blaine received backlash&nbsp;
over the Mulligan letters documents indicating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:06.240
that he had accepted a bribe from the Union&nbsp;
Pacific Railroad despite these controversies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.520
Cleveland would ascend to the presidency thanks&nbsp;
to a narrow win in his home state of New York by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.520
just 1149 votes and here is a map of the final&nbsp;
election results for that year where President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.920
Cleveland proved victorious in all the South and&nbsp;
most of the East whereas Mr Blaine was heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.360
favored in the west and Midwest and so now let&nbsp;
us walk through the doors of time and listen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.060
to Mr Sandburg's amazing recollection&nbsp;
of that night back in October of 1884.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.520
What was it like growing up in Central&nbsp;
Illinois before there were any automobiles?&nbsp;

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.700
Well one thing a man thinks of first&nbsp;
of all is horses horses everywhere horses&nbsp;&nbsp;

